# Maestri — skill gate (mandatory)

When `maestri` is on PATH (or `$MAESTRI_CLI` is set), you are likely inside the Maestri canvas. **Before** using Shell to debug another terminal, delegating work, driving a browser, or editing sticky notes, run this gate.

## Step 1 — Detect environment

```bash
command -v maestri >/dev/null 2>&1 || test -n "$MAESTRI_CLI"
```

If false, skip Maestri skills and work normally.

If true, run `maestri list` once per task (or when connections may have changed).

## Step 2 — Pick the skill (read SKILL.md before acting)

| User intent / signal | Skill to read |
|---|---|
| Check terminal output, dev server error, `pnpm dev` failed | `maestri-terminal` |
| Ask another agent, delegate, `check on`, collaborate | `maestri` |
| Shared notes: read, write, edit, create todos | `maestri-notes` |
| Browse URL, test UI, login flow, screenshot page | `maestri-portal` |
| Assemble team, recruit, connect agents, roles | `maestri-manager` |
| New workspace/floor, isolate branch experiment | `maestri-workspace` |
| Schedule recurring command or reminder | `maestri-routines` |
| Multiple Maestri concerns at once | Read `maestri` first, then the specific skill |

Project skills live in `.cursor/skills/maestri*/SKILL.md` (also `.claude/skills/`, `.opencode/skills/`).

## Step 3 — Execute with Maestri CLI

- Prefer `maestri check` over re-running `pnpm dev` when diagnosing a **connected** terminal.
- Prefer `maestri portal snapshot` over Playwright MCP when a **connected portal** exists.
- Use `maestri ask` only when another agent should do the work; use `maestri check` for read-only inspection.
- If `maestri` is missing from PATH: `"$MAESTRI_CLI" <subcommand> ...`

## Subagents

Include this gate in every subagent prompt that involves terminals, browsers, notes, or multi-agent work on the Maestri canvas.
